    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the membership level 4 tenure rule in the `membershipLevel` mapping expression in `OwnerMapper.java`.\n\n**Before**, level was capped at 3:\n```java\nMath.min(3, 1 + <email?1:0> + <namesakeCount==0?1:0>)\n```\n\n**After**, a tenure bonus can lift the level to 4, but only when tenure exceeds 365 days:\n```java\nMath.min(3, 1 + <email?1:0> + <namesakeCount==0?1:0>)\n  + (registrationDate != null\n     && ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(registrationDate, LocalDate.now()) > 365 ? 1 : 0)\n```\n\nKey points satisfying the spec:\n- **Level 4 requires tenure > 365 days** \u2014 the `+1` is only added when the days between `registrationDate` and today strictly exceed 365.\n- **A new owner never exceeds level 3** \u2014 a freshly created owner's `registrationDate` is set to today (zero tenure), so the tenure bonus is 0, leaving them at `min(3, ...)`. The pre-tenure factors (email present, `namesakeCount == 0`) max out at 3, which is exactly what the acceptance test `coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3` asserts.\n\nThe \"3-member household\" in the spec is descriptive context; household size was never a factor in the existing formula, so the pre-tenure ceiling of 3 is unchanged.",
